            According to Theorem \ref{thm:prelongation}, the system \eqref{eq:sys_quadcopter} together with the invertible, coordinate transformations \eqref{eq:step_1_trafo}, \eqref{eq:step_2_trafo}, \eqref{eq:step_1_trafo_1} and a $d_1=\#R_1-\#\lambda+m=2$-fold prelongation, i.e.
            \begin{equation*}
                \begin{aligned}
                    \bar\zeta^{1,+}_{1,[-2]} &= \bar\zeta^{1}_{1,[-1]} \\
                    \bar\zeta^{1,+}_{1,[-1]} &= \bar g^1_{1}(x^3,x^7) \\
                    x^{+} & =f(x, u)
                \end{aligned}
            \end{equation*}
            with the $n=12+2=14$-dimensional state $(\bar\zeta^{1}_{1,[-2]},\bar\zeta^{1}_{1,[-1]},x^1,\ldots,x^{12})$, original input $u$ and $\bar g^1_1=x^3-T_s x^7$, is static feedback linearizable. This can once again be verified using e.g. the static feedback linearization test of \cite{Grizzle:1986}.
